reports/
.env*

# ── B-6: POS snapshot durability (nagham.md B-6) ─────────────────────────────
# Snapshots — data/internal/snapshots/<ts>/{products,inventory}.parquet + meta.json
# — are the ONLY source of sales velocity: it is reconstructed from stock deltas
# between them (the POS export has no sales history). They must be a continuous,
# durable series, not live on one laptop and die with a reformat. They are tiny
# (~KB each), so we commit them to git. These negations must stay at the END of
# the file so they win over `data/internal/snapshots/` (above) and `*.parquet`.
# NOTE: this puts a real store's inventory data in git — keep the repo PRIVATE.
!data/internal/snapshots/
!data/internal/snapshots/**

# Playwright run output (e2e). Regenerated on every run.
test-results/
playwright-report/
# ── T7: receiving ledger (nagham.md T7) ───────────────────────────────────────
# data/internal/receiving/ holds two files, and both stay out of git, for two
# different reasons:
#   - supplier_lead_times.json is a regenerable build artifact — rerun
#     `npm run data:lead-times` and it comes back byte-for-byte from
#     receipts.csv. Same class as every other generated JSON above.
#   - receipts.csv is the append-only ledger of real deliveries and IS
#     irreplaceable (there is no upstream system to regenerate it from), but
#     unlike the B-6 snapshots above it carries the client's supplier names and
#     purchase costs — private commercial data, not public competitor prices.
#     Do not add a `!data/internal/receiving/` exception by analogy with B-6:
#     that precedent doesn't apply here. Durability is handled by an
#     out-of-band backup instead (see Task 8), not by committing it to git.
data/internal/receiving/

# Test caches.
.pytest_cache/

# UI audit output (screenshots + findings). Regenerated on demand.
audit/

# Playwright MCP browser session artifacts
.playwright-mcp/
